        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Harmonics are currents and voltages at integer multiples of the fundamental 50 or 60 Hz, riding on top of the ideal sine wave your equipment expects. On a modern low-voltage (LV) bus they are not an edge case but the default condition &mdash; produced by the very equipment that defines a contemporary plant: variable-frequency drives, rectifiers, UPS systems, LED lighting, and PV inverters. Left unchecked, they erode efficiency, age equipment, and push a facility past IEEE 519. This article explains what harmonics are, where they come from, and how a low-voltage active harmonic filter (AHF) cancels them.</p>

<h2 id="where-harmonics-come-from" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Where Harmonics Come From</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">A harmonic is simply a frequency at an integer multiple of the fundamental. A nonlinear load draws current that is not proportional to the applied voltage, so its current waveform is distorted &mdash; and Fourier analysis resolves that distortion into the fundamental plus a stack of harmonics.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">The classic source is a six-pulse rectifier &mdash; the front end of most VFDs and UPS units. It draws current in discrete lumps, generating the characteristic orders n = 6k &plusmn; 1: the 5th, 7th, 11th, and 13th. Left untreated, total demand distortion (THDi) at the load often sits between <strong>8% and 30%</strong> &mdash; well above the IEEE 519 recommendation of <strong>THDi &lt; 5%</strong> at the point of common coupling (PCC).</p>

<h2 id="why-harmonics-are-a-threat" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Harmonics Are a Threat</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed">
  <li><strong>Overheating</strong> &mdash; harmonic current raises copper loss with the square of RMS current and core loss with flux harmonics, so transformers and motors run hotter than their nameplate assumes.</li>
  <li><strong>Nuisance trips and breaker stress</strong> &mdash; the extra RMS current pushes protective devices toward operation.</li>
  <li><strong>Neutral overload</strong> &mdash; triplen harmonics (3rd, 9th, 15th) add in the neutral of a four-wire system and can exceed phase current.</li>
  <li><strong>Sensitive-load malfunction</strong> &mdash; PLCs, sensors, and servers misread a distorted bus.</li>
  <li><strong>Compliance and cost</strong> &mdash; exceed IEEE 519 and the utility can levy penalties or refuse a connection.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="why-an-ahf-not-a-fixed-filter" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why an AHF, Not a Fixed Filter</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">A passive LC trap is tuned to fixed orders and simply sits there. On a bus whose load changes by the minute, a fixed filter cannot track the load &mdash; and its shunt capacitance can resonate with grid impedance and <em>amplify</em> other harmonics.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">An active harmonic filter works the opposite way: it monitors the load continuously and injects a counter-current that cancels the distortion at the source.</p>

<h2 id="the-compensation-law" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">The Compensation Law</h2>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-brand-orange pl-4 italic text-text-mid my-6">i_c* = i_L &minus; i_s(fundamental)</blockquote>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Synthesized by a bridge switching at 15&ndash;20 kHz, that correction tracks within <strong>&lt; 1 ms</strong> and pulls THDi from <strong>30%+ down to &lt; 5%</strong> across the 5th through 25th orders at once &mdash; following the load as it varies. It also supplies reactive current and helps balance phases.</p>

<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic-the-thermal-ceiling" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC: The Thermal Ceiling</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Most LV AHFs use IGBTs with a junction capped near <strong>150 &deg;C</strong>. Silicon-carbide (SiC) MOSFETs widen the bandgap (<strong>3.26 eV vs 1.12 eV</strong>), tolerate <strong>200 &deg;C</strong>, switch at <strong>2&ndash;3&times;</strong> the frequency, and cut switching loss by roughly <strong>50%</strong> &mdash; a smaller, cooler cabinet with headroom for higher-order cancellation. That is the direction CHITEK's LV AHF platform is built on.</p>

<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6 pl-5 space-y-2">
  <li>Measure at the PCC with a power-quality analyzer over a full duty cycle.</li>
  <li>Place the AHF at the source &mdash; on the feeder that supplies the nonlinear cluster.</li>
  <li>Audit capacitor banks for resonance before commissioning.</li>
  <li>Size on measured TDD referenced to peak demand.</li>
  <li>Leave headroom (15&ndash;25%) for drives added later.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed">
  <li><strong>Industrial plants</strong> &mdash; VFDs dominate; the AHF restores &lt; 5% and cuts transformer heating.</li>
  <li><strong>Data centers</strong> &mdash; UPS rectifiers stack the 5th and 7th; the AHF protects uptime.</li>
  <li><strong>Commercial buildings</strong> &mdash; servers, LED, and HVAC drives; the AHF removes neutral overheating.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Harmonics come from the nonlinear loads you already run. A low-voltage <strong>AHF</strong> cancels them at the source &mdash; measurably, within IEEE 519. CHITEK's LV AHF platforms are specified from exactly this measurement-first discipline.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">What THDi is your bus actually logging &mdash; and is it below 5% at the PCC?</p>

